1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Qa4+ c6 5. Qxc4 Bf5 6. Nc3 e6 7. g3 Be7 8.
Bg2 Nbd7 9. Be3 ({White prepares a faulty manoevre, which is dealt with on
analogy with the "Mannheim trap" (pioneered by Alekhine).} 9. O-O {is
preferable.}) 9... O-O 10. Nh4 Bc2 11. a3 Nb6 12. Qa2 Ng4 13. b4 Nxe3 14. fxe3
Bxh4 15. Qxc2 Bg5 16. Qd3 Nd5 17. Nd1 ({Too passive; White can solve his
problems by} 17. Nxd5 {followed by e4.}) 17... a5 18. e4 ({Both here and the
next move I expected} 18. Rb1) 18... Nc7 19. bxa5 Nb5 20. e3 Qxa5+ 21. Kf2 Nxa3
22. Nb2 Qb4 23. Qe2 Qe7 24. h4 Bh6 25. g4 g6 ({Considering the game to be
positionally over, Black gets careless.} 25... f6 {was better.}) 26. g5 Bg7 27.
e5 Nb5 28. Nc4 Nc3 29. Qb2 Nd5 30. Nd6 $2 ({Overlooking a resource whereby
Black breaks free on the K Side. After} 30. Nd2 {aiming for e4 another way,
White may have sufficient play to balance the lost pawn.}) 30... Rxa1 31. Qxa1
f6 $1 32. Ke2 fxg5 33. h5 g4 34. hxg6 hxg6 35. Bxd5 cxd5 36. Qg1 Qg5 37. Qc1 ({
It is Black who has the attack; if} 37. Qh2 Rf3 38. Qh7+ Kf8 {. The white
knight at d6 cannot get back t«'defend the King.}) 37... g3 38. Rg1 Bh6 39.
Rg2 Qg4+ 40. Ke1 ({Or} 40. Kd3 Bxe3) 40... Qf3 41. Re2 g2 42. Kd2 Qf1 0-1

