1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 g6 3. d4 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Be2 O-O 6. Nf3 e5 7. d5 Nbd7 ({
This may be just a matter of taste, but it seems to me that the continuation} 
7... Na6 {which keeps open the c8-a3 diagonal, is here an easier form of
defence for Black.}) 8. Bg5 h6 9. Bh4 g5 10. Bg3 Nh5 11. h4 Nf4 {A sharp line,
at present in vogue. Owing to his seventh move Black does not come in time to
the strategic counter f5 and has - apart from the text continuation - only the
obscure and difficult variation 11...g4 at his disposal.} ({The immediate
capture} 11... Nxg3 12. fxg3 {followed up by 12...gxh4 is rather unpopular
since -Penrose's bad experience with it against Balashov at Hastings 66-67.})
12. hxg5 hxg5 13. Qc2 $1 {Reaching a critical position, generally assessed as
preferable for White. A view which this game is hardly going to change!} Nxg2+
({Much too risky. The more solid} 13... Nc5 14. b4 Na6 15. a3 {is Black's best
chance.}) 14. Kd2 $1 ({I prefer this to the also quite promising} 14. Kd1)
14... Qf6 $2 {This new attempt to "rehabilitate" the variation will be
drastically refuted. The black queen is helpless against the concentrated
onslaught of White's rooks and bishops.} ({White's idea is to bring as quickly
as possible the a1 rook to g1 even at the cost of a piece which White would
have to sacrifice after} 14... g4 15. Rag1 {(if the f3 knight moves, Qg5+!)}
gxf3 16. Bxf3 Nf4 17. Bxf4 exf4 18. e5 $1 {but of course with a very strong
attack.}) 15. Rag1 Nf4 16. Nxg5 $1 {Smashes open the g-file.} Bh6 ({If now (a)
} 16... Nxe2 17. Nh7 $1 Qe7 18. Kxe2 Re8 19. Qd3 {with an untenable position
for Black,}) ({(b)} 16... Qxg5 17. Bh4 Qh6 18. Bg5 Qg6 19. Bxf4 Qf6 20. Bh6 {
and wins.}) 17. Ke1 $1 {Moving quietly the king away from all possible checks
on the c1-h6 diagonal and threatening 18 Bxf4.} Ng6 ({After} 17... Bxg5 {comes}
18. Bh4 Ng6 19. Bxg5 Qg7 20. Bh6 {, or, perhaps better still, simmers the
helpless defender gently with 20 Bh5 or 20 Nb5.}) 18. Ne6 fxe6 ({After} 18...
Bg7 {White would have the choice between the simple 19 Nxf8 and - similarly to
the previous comment -} 19. Nxg7 Qxg7 20. Bh4 {or 20 Nb5 always with a
hopeless position for Black. After the text move the end follows rapidly.}) 19.
Rxh6 {Threatening Rxg6+ followed by Bh2.} Kf7 20. Bh5 Rg8 21. Bh4 {Source:
bulletin} (21. Bh4 {Black resigns. If} Qxh4 22. Bxg6+ Rxg6 23. dxe6+ $1 {
and White emerges from the complications with a queen against a minor piece of
Black. It is remarkable to note that the white queen except of the only modest
move Qc2 did not bother or need to join the struggle of her assistants.}) 1-0

