1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e6 6. Nf3 Be7 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8.
Bd3 O-O 9. O-O Nc6 10. Re1 Nxc3 ({Better} 10... Nf6 {or Bf6. The move played
gives d4 support and so enables White to start a kingside attack.}) 11. bxc3 b6
12. Qc2 g6 13. Bh6 Re8 14. Qd2 Bb7 15. h4 $1 Bf6 ({Black seeks central
counterplay by ...e5. 15 h4 is tactically justified by} 15... Bxh4 16. Nxh4
Qxh4 17. Bg5 Qg4 ({if} 17... Qh5 18. Be2 {wins the queen}) 18. Re4 Qf5 19. Rh4
Qd5 20. Be4 {with irresistible threats.}) 16. Bg5 $5 ({Pinning the bishop on
f6 and so preventing e5. More consistent, however, is} 16. h5 {meeting} e5 {by}
17. hxg6 hxg6 18. Be4) 16... Rc8 17. h5 $6 ({Overlooking that Black can now
play ...e5. Better is} 17. Qf4 Bxg5 18. Nxg5 {with strong pressure.}) 17... e5
18. hxg6 hxg6 19. Bb5 a6 $6 {Provoking complications, which turn out to be
unfavourable for Black, but Black's position is difficult anyway.} 20. Bxf6 $1
({Not} 20. Ba4 $2 b5 21. Bb3 b4 $1 {and White's position crumbles.}) 20... Qxf6
21. dxe5 Nxe5 $6 {Any queen move leaves White a solid pawn ahead.} 22. Bxe8
Nxf3+ ({If} 22... Bxf3 23. Rxe5 Qxe5 24. Bxf7+ Kxf7 25. Qd7+) ({or it} 22...
Qxf3 $5 23. Qg5 $1 (23. gxf3 $2 Nxf3+ 24. Kg2 Nxd2+ 25. Kh3 Rxc3+ {with two
pawns and a strong attack for the exchange.})) 23. gxf3 Qxf3 24. Bxf7+ $1 Kh7
$2 ({A mistake losing rapidly.} 24... Kxf7 {is best, but after} 25. Qd7+ {
White should still win, i.e.} Kg8 (25... Kf6 26. Re6+) (25... Kf8 26. Qd6+ Kg8
(26... Kf7 27. Re7+) 27. Qxg6+) 26. Re8+ Rxe8 27. Qxe8+ Kh7 28. Qe7+ Kg8 29.
Qd8+ Kg7 30. Qc7+ Kg8 31. Qh2 {and after suitable checks, the white queen
reaches h2 when White will be able to evaluate his extra exchange.}) 25. Bxg6+
$1 Kg8 26. Bh7+ $1 Kxh7 27. Re7+ {Source: bulletin} 1-0

