1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Bg5 a6 8.
Na3 ({At least as good as} 8. Bxf6) 8... b5 9. Nd5 Qa5+ 10. Bd2 Qd8 11. Bg5 Be7
{Not wanting such a quick draw.} 12. Bxf6 Bxf6 13. c4 Qa5+ $6 (13... b4 14. Nc2
Rb8 {is sounder and quite playable.}) 14. Qd2 {Obligatory.} Qxd2+ 15. Kxd2 b4 {
Consistent. If White takes the exchange, Black with Bg5+ and bxa3 gets strong
minor pieces and some initiative with f5. Whilst the theoretical adequacy is
questionable to say the least the sacrifice should give good practical chances.
} 16. Nc2 {Playing safe.} Bg5+ 17. Nce3 O-O 18. Bd3 f5 19. Nb6 (19. exf5 Bxf5 {
would be more prudent, though Black's active position at least compensates for
his d5 weakness.}) 19... fxe4 20. Bxe4 ({If} 20. Nxa8 Rxf2+ 21. Be2 ({or} 21.
Ke1 Bxe3 22. Bxe4 Nd4 {threatening Bg4 and Bb7}) 21... Bg4 22. Rae1 Nd4 {, etc}
) ({Possibly best would have been} 20. Rhf1 {when Black has the prosaic ...Rb8
or the more ambitious} exd3 21. Nxa8 Bg4 22. f3 Nd4) 20... Rb8 21. Bd5+ Kh8 22.
Nxc8 {The only way to avoid losing the f-pawn for nothing.} Rxf2+ 23. Ke1 ({If
} 23. Kd3 {simply} Rxc8) 23... Bxe3 24. Nxd6 Nd4 {Even with only these pieces
Black's attack will he ample compensation for a rook.} 25. Nf7+ Kg8 (25... Rxf7
26. Bxf7 Nc2+ 27. Ke2 Nxa1 28. Kxe3 {is bad.}) 26. Nxe5+ Kf8 27. Nd7+ Ke8 ({Not
} 27... Ke7 {which allows an eventual knight check on c6.}) 28. Nxb8 Re2+ 29.
Kd1 $2 (29. Kf1 {was essential. Then besides the perpetual Black has
fascinating possibilities, for example:} Rxb2 30. Re1 ({Stockfish 16:} 30. h4
$1 $18) 30... Rf2+ 31. Kg1 Ne2+ 32. Rxe2 Rxe2+ 33. Kf1 Rf2+ 34. Ke1 Rxa2 35. c5
({Stockfish 16:} 35. Rf1 $1 $18) 35... Ra1+ 36. Ke2 {(at which point the
annotator gives 36...P-N7, i.e. 36...b2, which is not legal - perhaps he was
looking at the wrong position?)}) 29... Rd2+ 30. Kc1 ({If} 30. Ke1 Nc2+ 31. Kf1
Rf2+ 32. Kg1 Rf3#) 30... Ne2+ 31. Kb1 Bd4 0-1

