1. e4 c5 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. Nf3 e6 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 d6 6. Be3 Nf6 7. Bc4 Be7 8.
Qe2 Qc7 9. Bb3 O-O 10. O-O-O a6 11. g4 Na5 ({More usual is} 11... Nxd4 12. Rxd4
e5 13. Rc4 ({A rediscovered move} 13. Nd5 {is at present felt to give White a
slight advantage}) 13... Qd8 {with a complex game.}) 12. g5 ({The alternative
is} 12. Kb1 {to be able to recapture with the c-pawn.}) 12... Nxb3+ 13. axb3
Nd7 14. Rhg1 (14. f4 {or 14 h4 are alternatives with promise.}) 14... b5 ({
In my opinion} 14... Re8 $1 {is the most flexible move.}) 15. Nf5 $5 exf5 (
15... Nc5 {is also playable e.g.} 16. Nxe7+ Qxe7 17. Bf4 Rd8 18. b4 {with a
small advantage for White.}) 16. Nd5 Qd8 17. exf5 Re8 18. f6 Bf8 19. fxg7 Be7
$2 (19... Bxg7 {was correct when White is hard pressed to justify his
sacrifice. The intended} 20. Qh5 ({Nor does} 20. Nf6+ Nxf6 (20... Bxf6 $5) 21.
gxf6 Qxf6 22. Bd4 Rxe2 23. Bxf6 Bf5 $1 {offer white winning chances.}) 20...
Bb7 21. Nf6+ Nxf6 22. gxf6 Qxf6 23. Bd4 ({Consequently White must play} 23. Bh6
{when Black apparently has nothing better than to take the perpetual check on
the long diagonal. However, Stockfish 16:} Qxb2+ 24. Kd2 Qc3+ 25. Kc1 Be4 26.
Rxg7+ Kh8 $19) {fails to the simple} 23... Re5) 20. Qh5 Ne5 21. f4 Ng6 22. Bb6
$1 {This prevents Black playing Bf5 which would be strong.} Qd7 23. Rde1 $1 {
And now Black has no answer to the threat Rxe7 followed by Nf6+.} Qh3 24. Nf6+
Kxg7 25. Nxe8+ Kf8 26. Qxh3 Bxh3 {And Black resigned when White safely reached
the time control at move 48. Amusing if not very deep or accurate. Source:
bulletin} 1-0

