{"I was rather pleased with my game in the last round, which I won. It is an
example of drastic punishment meted out to Black, who indulged in a rather
premature and reckless advance of his queenside pawns. There were some curious
aspects to the game. White took a reasonable time over his moves (1 hour 10
minutes for the whole game), whereas Black moved almost instantaneously (only
23 minutes in all), and was often away from the board when it was White's turn
to move. This gave me the impression at first that my opponent knew the
opening well! After I had made my 24th move, I got up and walked away from the
board, so that my opponent could see that I had moved, for he was not sitting
opposite me at the time. Presently, from afar, I saw my opponent walking away
from the table, and I went over to my place to see what he had played. I
studied the position, but could find no move on his part. I then noticed that
the clock had been stopped, and on my scoresheet, in the place for Black’s
24th move, had been written the word "Resigns"! My opponent had vanished, and
I never saw him again! Perhaps he had a train to catch!" (Kevin Claudius)} 1.
d4 e6 2. Nf3 d5 3. c3 c5 4. Nbd2 Nc6 5. e3 Nf6 6. Bd3 c4 7. Bc2 b5 8. O-O a5 9.
e4 b4 10. Ba4 Qc7 11. exd5 exd5 12. Ne5 Bb7 13. Nxc6 Bxc6 14. Re1+ Be7 15.
Bxc6+ Qxc6 16. Nf3 Ra7 17. Ne5 Qb7 18. Qa4+ Nd7 19. Nc6 Nb6 20. Qb5 Nc8 21.
Nxa7+ Qxb5 22. Nxb5 O-O 23. Re5 Re8 24. Nc7 {Source: 1971 Islington bulletin 
(not the 1970 one)} 1-0

