1. b4 {This came as a slight surprise; I had expected 1 e4. However a lack of
knowledge proved to be no handicap since it seems clear that Black should
strive for a kingside initiative as a counter.} e5 2. Bb2 d6 3. b5 {This must
be premature.} Be6 4. e3 Nd7 5. c4 f5 6. Nf3 {Bellin, observing our
post-mortem, suggested a fianchetto of the King’s Bishop. Dennis spent a
long time over this move and explained that, in some variations, White’s f4
becomes available for the knight. A drawback to the move chosen is that it
renders f4 impossible, thus permitting my reply.} Be7 7. Be2 Nh6 ({I felt that
this was the simplest course. If} 7... Ngf6 8. Ng5) 8. d4 Bf6 9. Nc3 e4 10. Nd2
c6 $2 ({Intending to answer 11...f4 with 11...exf3 and then 12...d5.} 10... O-O
{(it gives 10...0-0-0 in the text - JS) would have been better, as f4 would be
answered in the seme way with a kingside attack.}) 11. a4 $2 ({10...c6? permits
} 11. bxc6 bxc6 12. Qa4 Qc7 13. d5 cxd5 14. cxd5 Bf7 (14... Bxc3 15. Bxc3 {
threatening both Bxg7 and dxe6... JS note, this doesn't make much sense}) 15.
Rc1 {with a strong attack.}) 11... O-O 12. Ba3 $2 {Taking the bishop off its
best diagonal and forcing me to block the queenside.} c5 13. Rc1 Qe7 14. Nb3
Rac8 $1 {This move might be cited as an example of Nimzovitchian
overprotection. Now 15 Na5 Nb6 merely increases Black's pre­ssure. The
maneouvre, by means of which White eliminates my black-squared bishop in the
next few moves, is a natural one as it gives him the long diagonal back. But
this bishop never got there as my attack materialised out of nowhere, to the
surprise of both parties.} 15. dxc5 dxc5 16. Nd5 Qf7 17. Nxf6+ Qxf6 18. Qc2 {
This prevents f4 due to the attack on the e4-pawn and threatens Bb2.} Qg6 19.
g3 ({If} 19. O-O f4 20. exf4 Bh3 21. g3 {or 21 Bf3 then} Rxf4 {wins.}) 19...
Ng4 20. h3 $2 Nxe3 21. fxe3 ({No calculation is now required for the ensuing
sacrifice, other than} 21. Qc3 Ng2+ 22. Kf1 f4 {is sufficient. Ng4 was part of
a plan to recentralise and to exploit the white squares.}) 21... Qxg3+ 22. Kd2
Rfd8 23. Qc3 ({In view of the threat Nb6+ and Nxa4 mate. If} 23. Rcf1 Ne5+ 24.
Kc1 {(or 34 Kc3)} Qxe3+ 25. Kb2 Rd4 {winning.}) ({If} 23. Na1 Ne5+ 24. Kc3
Qxe3+ 25. Kb2 Rd2) 23... Nb6+ 24. Kc2 Nxa4 25. Qa1 Qxe3 26. Rce1 Rd3 $1 27.
Bxd3 ({"If BB1 BxP wins." Both ambiguous and wrong: whether the annotator
means 23 Bc1 or 23 Bf1, 23...Bxc4 loses for Black. The notes to this game are
hard to figure out. JS... If} 27. Bh5 Rc3+ 28. Kd1 Rd8+) ({If} 27. Bd1 Rc3+ 28.
Kb1 Rxb3+ {transposes into the game.}) ({If} 27. Bb2 Rxb3 28. Qxa4 Rd8 {
shatters all resistance.}) 27... Qxd3+ 28. Kc1 Qxb3 29. Rhg1 Qxc4+ {Source:
bulletin. The score seems correct but some of the notes don't make sense. JS} (
29... Qxc4+ 30. Kb1 Qxb5+ {with six pawns for the exchange, a record! Mate is
soon forced anyway with the N on a2, Q on b3 and K [on b2].}) 0-1

