1. e4 c6 2. c4 d5 3. cxd5 cxd5 4. exd5 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nbd7 6. Nf3 (6. d6 {, forcing
} e6 {, may be better.}) 6... Nb6 7. d4 g6 8. Bd3 Bg7 9. O-O O-O 10. Bg5 ({
Better is} 10. Be3) 10... Nfxd5 {To obtain pressure on c4.} 11. Qd2 Nxc3 12.
bxc3 {White now has hanging pawns.} Be6 13. Rfe1 {Indirectly protecting c4.}
Rc8 14. Ne5 $5 {A positional sacrifice. In compensation for the piece lost,
White obtains two pawns and Black’s kingside is shattered. The pawn on c3
remains weak, however.} f6 15. Nxg6 hxg6 16. Rxe6 fxg5 17. Qxg5 ({To obtain
pressure on e7 and g6. Objectively} 17. Rxg6 {was better as the c3-pawn is
then still protected.}) (17. Bxg6 {is bad as} Rf6 18. Rae1 Rxe6 19. Rxe6 Bf6 {
leaves Black nothing to fear.}) 17... Rxc3 $1 {Very strong, as Qxd4 is now
threatened which would not only put pressure on f2 but also protect the bishop.
} 18. Qxg6 Rf6 $2 (18... Qxd4 {is also bad:} 19. Qh7+ Kf7 20. Rae1 {wins}) ({
As Black is in difficulties on the light squares, correct was} 18... Rxd3 $1
19. Qxd3 Qxd4 20. Qxd4 Bxd4 21. Rf1 {and Black has good chances in the ending
and certainly should not lose.}) 19. Rxf6 exf6 20. Re1 Kf8 ({Clearly} 20...
Rxd3 21. Qxd3 {is now not nearly so good.} (21. Re8+ {is better - JS})) 21. Bb5
{Black overlooked this and is in a crushing bind. There is practically no
counterplay so White's strategy is to march his h-pawn to h6.} Nd7 22. h4 Rc8
23. h5 Qa5 24. Rb1 Qc7 25. h6 Nb6 26. Re1 {Better than Qxg7+ or hxg7+
immediately.} Qf7 27. hxg7+ Kg8 28. Qh6 Qxa2 $2 ({White also wins after} 28...
Qxg7 29. Re8+ Rxe8 30. Qxg7+ Kxg7 31. Bxe8 {but this hastens the end
considerably.}) 29. Be8 {Source: bulletin} 1-0

